  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nHot and dry conditions will continue for Friday through the middle \nof next week as an upper-level ridge is expected to remain the \ndominant feature across the southern US.  By Friday the center of \nthe ridge will shift to the Desert Southwest while becoming \nelongated over the southern Plains and Gulf Coast.  This will occur \nas the middle of the ridge will become \"pinched\" between two \nfeatures: A shortwave trough over Oklahoma and the circulation of \nTropical Storm Bertha over south Texas.  The effect of these two \nfeatures will lower mid-level heights and introduce more cloud cover \ninto west central Texas on Friday, which would potentially lower \ntemperatures by several degrees.  The \"pinching\" features should \nmove out of the picture on Saturday and allow the ridge to re-expand \nand become re-centered over the Rockies.  Models show the ridge \nremaining in place through at least early next week with west \ncentral Texas in the southeast quadrant of the ridge.  This should \nallow for hot and dry conditions to continue through the middle of \nnext week. High temperatures should range from the upper 90s to the \nlow 100s during this time.  The warmest conditions are most likely \nto occur across the Big Country where the highest mid-level heights \nwill be located.",
